(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-cam-nutrition-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CAM/Nutrition Exam 1 — Master Exam (Form A)","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The lecture uses the term \"sarcopenic obesity\" to describe which combination?","choices":["Bone loss combined with dehydration","Advanced muscle loss accompanied by gains in fat mass","High muscle mass with very low body fat","Loss of both muscle and fat during illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: sarcopenic obesity is advanced muscle loss occurring together with gains in fat mass. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Fiber is classified within which of the lecture's six classes of nutrients?","choices":["Amino acid","Trace metal","Carbohydrate","Fatty acid"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture lists glycogen, starch, and fiber together as complex carbohydrates, so fiber falls under the carbohydrate class of nutrients. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 13 (Carbohydrates)"},{"q":"Per lecture, specialized nutrition support (SNS) is generally considered in adults after how many days without adequate intake?","choices":["More than 30 days","5 to 7 days","Fewer than 1 day","1 to 3 days"],"answer":1,"exp":"SNS is generally considered after more than 5-7 days in adults (compared to 3-5 days in children and 1-3 days in infants). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Per the lecture, fasting hyperglycemia is defined as a blood glucose above what level?","choices":["Greater than 60 grams per day","Exactly 100 milligrams per deciliter in all people","Greater than 125 milligrams per 100 milliliters of blood","Below 40 milligrams per 100 milliliters"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: fasting hyperglycemia is greater than 125 milligrams per 100 milliliters of blood. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 32"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish a food allergy from a food intolerance?","choices":["A food allergy never involves the immune system","A food intolerance is always more severe than an allergy","The two terms mean exactly the same thing","A food allergy involves an immune response, whereas a food intolerance does not"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a food allergy involves an immune response, while a food intolerance is a reaction that does not involve an allergic (immune) mechanism. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 601"},{"q":"Which type of food allergy reaction does the lecture describe as often presenting soon after ingestion, being more violent, and potentially causing anaphylaxis?","choices":["Non-IgE mediated (late onset)","IgE mediated (early onset)","Cross-reactive syndrome","Delayed hypersensitivity syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states IgE mediated (early onset) reactions 'often present soon after digestion,' are 'often more violent,' and can include anaphylaxis. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A woman with pregestational diabetes has poorly controlled hyperglycemia during weeks 3-8 of embryonic development. Why does the lecture flag this window as especially concerning?","choices":["This is when the fetus doubles in length and increases most in weight, matching the lecture's own description of the third-trimester fetal growth patterns.","This is the period of organogenesis, when major organs are forming, and maternal hyperglycemia during this window is associated with an increased risk of congenital malformations.","This is the period when iron intake takes priority for the developing fetus, according to the lecture's description of fetal needs.","This is when pregnancy weight gain should be at its fastest pace, roughly 1 pound gained per week, according to the lecture."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states organogenesis occurs during weeks 3-8, and maternal hyperglycemia during this critical period is associated with increased risk of congenital malformations because excess glucose crosses the placenta and can disrupt embryonic development. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Vitamin K is required as a coenzyme in the synthesis of proteins involved in which two processes, per the slide?","choices":["Calcium absorption and phosphorus balance","Blood clotting and bone metabolism","Antioxidant defense and fatty acid breakdown prevention","Vision and gene expression"],"answer":1,"exp":"The slide states vitamin K is required as a coenzyme during the synthesis of proteins involved in blood clotting and bone metabolism. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 100 (Vitamins: Classification & Function—Fat Soluble)"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish simple from complex carbohydrates?","choices":["Simple carbohydrates are monosaccharides and disaccharides; complex carbohydrates include glycogen, starch, and fiber","Simple carbohydrates provide 9 kilocalories per gram and complex provide 4","Simple carbohydrates are inorganic and complex are organic","Complex carbohydrates cannot be used for energy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: simple carbohydrates are mono- and disaccharides, while complex carbohydrates are glycogen, starch, and fiber. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 13"},{"q":"How does the lecture define basal metabolic rate (BMR)?","choices":["The calories stored as adipose tissue","The number of calories in a typical older adult's meal","The total calories the body needs to perform basic life-sustaining functions","The calories burned only during physical exercise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: basal metabolic rate is the total number of calories needed for basic life-sustaining functions. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"The lecture notes that among infants and children who fail to grow as expected, what share have no apparent underlying disease?","choices":["About 5%","About 30%","About 50%","About 80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: about 80% of children with failure to thrive have no apparent disease, pointing to environmental factors. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"On a growth chart, what does a child's percentile represent?","choices":["The percentage of body weight that is fat","The percentage of calories from fat in the diet","The number of teeth the child has erupted","The child's rank among 100 peers matched for age and gender"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a percentile is the rank among 100 peers of the same age and gender, with the 50th percentile considered average. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How does the lecture define a functional food, and what example does it give?","choices":["A food engineered to contain no calories","A prepackaged meal replacement shake","Any food eaten primarily for its flavor","A food providing health benefits beyond its traditional nutrients, such as tomatoes containing lycopene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: functional foods provide benefits beyond traditional nutrients; the lecture cites tomatoes' phytochemical lycopene. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 45"},{"q":"If calorie intake is 3,000 kcal, roughly how many of those kcal does the thermic effect of food (TEF) account for, per lecture's example?","choices":["150 to 300 kcal","1,500 kcal","900 to 1,200 kcal","15 to 30 kcal"],"answer":0,"exp":"At 3,000 kcal intake, TEF (roughly 5-10% of intake) would account for about 150 to 300 kcal, per lecture's own worked example. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why does the lecture advise pregnant patients to avoid eating liver and to avoid taking excess prenatal vitamins, especially in the first trimester?","choices":["Liver is a major source of saturated fat, which should be limited to under 10% of total daily calories.","Liver can be a source of foodborne illness such as listeria if it is undercooked.","Liver is very high in iodine content, which can cause fetal goiter in excess.","Excessive vitamin A intake increases the risk of a teratogenic effect, and liver is a concentrated source of vitamin A."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states excessive vitamin A intake increases teratogenic risk and specifically advises avoiding liver, especially in the first trimester, for this reason. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"According to the 2018 NHANES data cited in lecture, which macronutrient's share of kilocalorie intake (35.8%) slightly exceeded the top end of the Food and Nutrition Board's advised range?","choices":["Fat","Carbohydrate","Protein","Alcohol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fat accounted for 35.8% of kcal intake against an advised range of 20%–35%, putting the North American average slightly above the top of the recommended range. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 32 (Assessing the Current North American Eating Pattern)"},{"q":"If ascites is present in a chronic liver disease patient, what sodium intake is recommended, per lecture?","choices":["No sodium restriction is needed.","Restrict sodium to under 100 mg/day.","Increase sodium intake to compensate for fluid loss.","Reduce sodium intake to 1500-2000 mg/day."],"answer":3,"exp":"If ascites is present, sodium intake should be reduced to 1500-2000 mg/day (and fluid restriction may also be required). — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"According to the ACOG guidance cited in the lecture, what daily caffeine intake does not appear to be a major contributing factor to miscarriage or preterm birth?","choices":["Any amount, since caffeine has no known effects in pregnancy","Less than 500 mg/day","Less than 200 mg/day","Caffeine should be avoided completely, with zero tolerance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states ACOG considers moderate caffeine consumption of less than 200 mg/day not to be a major contributing factor to miscarriage or preterm birth. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Why does chronically low protein intake lead to edema, according to the slide on protein and fluid balance?","choices":["Low protein directly increases capillary blood pressure, forcing fluid out of vessels and into surrounding tissue spaces","Protein deficiency increases sodium retention, which then osmotically pulls extra water out of the bloodstream and into tissues","Low protein intake causes the kidneys to stop filtering fluid entirely, so waste and water both build up systemically","Protein molecules are too large to move into tissues, so with too little protein, fluid isn't pulled back into the bloodstream and builds up in tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Protein molecules are normally too large to move out of the bloodstream into tissue, and that size difference is what lets protein pull the proper amount of fluid back into the blood. When protein intake is too low, that pull weakens, fluid builds up in the tissues, and edema results. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 80 (Protein Maintains Fluid Balance)"},{"q":"Which increased health risk did the Dutch Famine research, as summarized in the lecture, associate with exposure specifically during the second trimester of gestation?","choices":["Breast cancer","Stress sensitivity","Obstructive lung disease","Blood lipid profile associated with atherosclerosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists obstructive lung disease under second-trimester exposure, along with kidney disease and glucose intolerance. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the average daily calorie need range given for women, per lecture?","choices":["1,800 to 2,400 kcal","1,200 to 1,600 kcal","3,500 to 4,000 kcal","2,400 to 3,200 kcal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Average daily calorie need for women is 1,800 to 2,400 kcal per day, per lecture. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"According to lecture, what degree of lean body mass loss is associated with impaired wound healing?","choices":["15-20% loss","Loss of lean body mass does not affect wound healing.","5% loss","10% loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"A 15-20% loss of lean body mass will impair wound healing. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does the lecture state causes heartburn?","choices":["Stomach acid backing up into the esophagus","Infection caused by H. pylori bacteria","Overuse of NSAID pain medications","Flattened villi within the small intestine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states heartburn results from stomach acid backing up into the esophagus. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"The American Academy of Pediatrics recommendation cited in the lecture calls for how much vitamin D per day for all infants and children under 1 year?","choices":["100 international units per day","1,000 international units per day","No vitamin D is recommended in infancy","400 international units per day"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: infants and children under 1 year are recommended 400 international units of vitamin D per day. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"Which of the following herbal supplements does the lecture specifically flag as a concern during pregnancy?","choices":["Ginger","Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Peppermint","Black cohosh"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture lists black cohosh among the herbal supplements of particular concern during pregnancy. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as a symptom of a late-onset (non-IgE mediated) food reaction?","choices":["Urticaria and hives","Wheezing and cough","Food-sensitive colitis","Sudden anaphylaxis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists food-sensitive colitis under late-onset reactions to food. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"The lecture lists Loma Linda, California as a Blue Zone, noting it has a large concentration of which group?","choices":["Seventh-Day Adventists","Professional athletes","Centenarian farmers from Okinawa","Mediterranean fishermen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Loma Linda is noted for its large concentration of Seventh-Day Adventists. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Saturated fatty acids are found primarily in which type of food, and what physical state do they typically take at room temperature?","choices":["Plant foods; solid","Animal fats; liquid","Animal fats; solid","Plant foods; liquid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Saturated fatty acids are found primarily in animal fats and take a solid form at room temperature. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 49 (Fatty Acids and Triglycerides)"},{"q":"Chromium is classified as a trace mineral whose primary listed role, per the slide, is:","choices":["Blood clotting and muscle contraction","Antioxidant defense against oxidative stress","Helping maintain normal blood glucose levels","Component of thyroid hormones"],"answer":2,"exp":"The slide lists chromium's role as helping to maintain normal blood glucose levels, distinguishing it from the other trace minerals discussed nearby (copper, fluoride, manganese, molybdenum). — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 112 (Minerals Classification & Function: Trace Minerals)"},{"q":"How does the Bod Pod® measure body composition, per lecture?","choices":["By sending a small painless electrical current through the entire body","By pinching and measuring skinfold thickness at several sites with calipers","By determining body volume and air displaced in a sealed chamber (air displacement)","By performing a full-body dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry scan"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Bod Pod® uses air displacement — determining body volume and air displaced in a sealed chamber — to calculate body density (body weight per body volume). — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What distinguishes simple starvation from stress starvation?","choices":["Simple starvation is defined as a condition that only affects pediatric patients; stress starvation is defined as a condition that only affects elderly adults with multiple comorbidities.","Simple starvation is characterized by dramatic electrolyte shifts into cells; stress starvation is characterized by the complete absence of any electrolyte changes.","Simple starvation occurs when a healthy individual stops eating; stress starvation occurs when a starved individual is also subject to the metabolic response of trauma, sepsis, or critical illness.","Simple starvation is always fatal within days regardless of treatment; stress starvation is always fully reversible once feeding resumes."],"answer":2,"exp":"Simple starvation is a healthy individual simply not eating; stress starvation adds the metabolic response of trauma, sepsis, or critical illness on top of inadequate intake. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What typically happens to the composition of weight lost during the early phase of a weight-loss plan, per lecture?","choices":["Early weight loss is larger and includes fluid and fat.","No measurable weight loss occurs during the early phase of a new diet.","Weight loss is perfectly linear throughout a diet.","Early weight loss consists purely of muscle mass."],"answer":0,"exp":"Early weight loss is larger and includes both fluid and fat; the rate begins to slow as muscle mass increases with continued exercise. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What does the lecture identify as the cause of heartburn?","choices":["Bacterial overgrowth in the small intestine","Stomach acid backing up into the esophagus","Delayed gastric emptying","Inflammation of the gallbladder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states heartburn 'results from stomach acid backing up into esophagus.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"The lecture states that a declining basal metabolic rate can reduce a 70-year-old man's daily calorie need by roughly how much, yet intake often stays the same, leading to obesity?","choices":["About 500 to 700 kilocalories","About 1,000 kilocalories","There is no reduction with age","About 100 to 150 kilocalories"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the lecture cites a reduction of about 100 to 150 kilocalories in daily need for a 70-year-old man. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which fat-soluble vitamin is required as a coenzyme in the synthesis of proteins involved in blood clotting and bone metabolism?","choices":["Vitamin D","Vitamin A","Vitamin E","Vitamin K"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vitamin K is required as a coenzyme during synthesis of proteins involved in blood clotting and bone metabolism. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 100 (Vitamins: Classification & Function — Fat Soluble)"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a resource to help combat food insecurity, per lecture?","choices":["OSHA workplace safety grant funding programs","Medicare Part D prescription drug coverage","WIC (Women, Infants, and Children program)","FEMA emergency disaster relief loan programs"],"answer":2,"exp":"WIC is listed among the resources to combat undernutrition/food insecurity, along with Summer EBT, school lunch/breakfast programs, the Child and Adult Care Food Program, and afterschool nutrition programs. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What is the recommended rate of weight loss in a sound weight-loss plan, per lecture?","choices":["0.5 to 1 pound every 2 weeks","As fast as possible, regardless of rate","1 to 2 pounds per week","5 pounds per week"],"answer":2,"exp":"A sound weight-loss plan encourages slow and steady loss of 1 to 2 pounds per week, rather than rapid weight loss. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which combination of symptoms is specifically marked in the lecture as indicating very rapid, potentially fatal anaphylaxis?","choices":["Itching and hives on the skin","Nausea and bloating in the abdomen","Runny nose and nasal congestion","Low blood pressure and rapid heart rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture marks both of these cardiovascular symptoms with an asterisk indicating very rapid, potentially fatal anaphylaxis. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as a cause of hemorrhoids?","choices":["H. pylori infection","Poorly digested carbohydrates","Prolonged sitting","Gluten exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists prolonged sitting as a stress factor causing hemorrhoids. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does the lecture define minerals?","choices":["Compounds easily destroyed by heat during cooking","The body's main structural building material","Inorganic substances that contain no carbon atoms, are not destroyed by cooking, and provide no calories","Organic compounds that provide 4 kilocalories per gram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: minerals are inorganic (no carbon), are not destroyed during cooking, and produce no calories. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 19"},{"q":"Per lecture, how has increasing affluence in North America contributed to leading causes of death?","choices":["It has led to decreased alcohol consumption, reduced use of processed foods, and less reliance on convenience dining.","It has led to increased physical activity, reduced sugar intake, and more consistent structured exercise habits.","It has led to increased consumption of fiber, whole grains, and other minimally processed staple foods.","It has led to more sedentary lifestyles and higher intakes of animal fat, sugar, salt, and alcohol."],"answer":3,"exp":"Lecture notes that although North Americans live longer with more diverse food and lifestyle choices, affluence has also driven more sedentary lifestyles and higher intakes of animal fat, sugar, salt, and alcohol — factors that feed into leading causes of death. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 42 (Longer Healthier Lives)"},{"q":"The lecture notes vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is a special concern in older adults because its absorption declines with age for what reason?","choices":["The kidneys excrete it more rapidly","It is destroyed by weight-bearing exercise","It binds to dietary fiber and is lost","Low stomach acid secretion reduces its absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: vitamin B12 absorption declines with age due to reduced stomach acid secretion. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What BMI category does a BMI of 18.50-24.99 fall into, per the categories given?","choices":["Overweight (pre-obese)","Underweight","Obese class I","Normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"A BMI of 18.50-24.99 falls into the Normal category. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which malnutrition-related lab marker is specifically noted as being affected by iron deficiency and inflammation, with a half-life of 8-10 days?","choices":["Prealbumin","Transferrin","Retinol binding protein","Albumin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Transferrin levels are affected by iron deficiency and inflammation, with a half-life of 8-10 days. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The lecture lists nutrition interventions sometimes used for children with autism spectrum disorder. Which matches?","choices":["Exclusive breast milk through age 5","A gluten-free, casein-free diet with supplements such as probiotics and omega-3 fatty acids","A high-sodium, high-sugar diet","Complete elimination of all protein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: interventions listed include a gluten-free, casein-free diet and supplements such as probiotics, vitamin B6, magnesium, and omega-3 fatty acids. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 413"},{"q":"The lecture lists a fading sensitivity to the thirst sensation as one reason older adults may have low fluid intake. What daily fluid target does it suggest?","choices":["About six to eight 8-ounce glasses daily","About one 8-ounce glass daily","About sixteen 8-ounce glasses daily","Fluid intake does not need to be monitored"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the lecture suggests aiming for six to eight 8-ounce glasses of water daily. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what share of young children outgrow food allergies before 3 years of age?","choices":["About 10%","About 40%","Almost none outgrow them","About 80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: about 80% of young children outgrow food allergies before 3 years of age. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 657"},{"q":"The lecture cites an American Academy of Pediatrics recommendation to limit caffeine for adolescents to what amount, if any?","choices":["1,000 milligrams per day","There is no recommended limit","100 milligrams per day, if at all","500 milligrams per day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the recommendation is to limit caffeine to 100 milligrams per day, if any. — PAJ5508 Infancy Through Adolescence mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 562"},{"q":"Per the lecture, how does inadequate protein intake lead to edema?","choices":["Low protein raises blood glucose, drawing water into tissues","Proteins normally attract fluid back into the bloodstream; when protein is too low, fluid builds up in the tissues","Low protein increases red blood cell production, flooding tissues","Excess protein blocks the kidneys, causing swelling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: blood proteins pull fluid back into vessels, so low protein lets fluid accumulate in tissues as edema. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 80"},{"q":"Which hormone is responsible for the 'let-down reflex,' or milk ejection/release, during breastfeeding?","choices":["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states breastmilk ejection or release is promoted by oxytocin, described as the let-down reflex. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which hormone is responsible for the 'let-down reflex,' the ejection or release of breastmilk?","choices":["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct -- the lecture states oxytocin promotes breastmilk ejection or release, known as the let-down reflex. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is a key difference between gastric feedings and post-pyloric feedings in enteral nutrition?","choices":["Post-pyloric feedings never require a pump and can be safely delivered as rapid bolus doses several times daily.","Post-pyloric feedings are always more physiologic than gastric feedings and carry a lower aspiration risk overall.","Gastric feedings can be given as bolus feeds, while post-pyloric feedings must run slowly via pump and should NOT be bolused.","Gastric feedings cannot be used for medication administration and must always run continuously via pump."],"answer":2,"exp":"Gastric feedings (GT/PEG/NGT) are the most physiologic and can be given as bolus feeds; post-pyloric feedings (NJT/NDT/GJT/PEJ) must run slowly via a pump over up to 24 hours and should NOT be bolused. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"According to the lecture, although adults over 65 years make up less than 15% of the United States population, what share of all prescription medications, hospitalizations, and the federal health budget do they account for?","choices":["About 80%","About 35%","About 15%","About 55%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the lecture states people over 65 account for about 35% of all prescription medications, hospitalizations, and the federal health budget. — Nutrition & Aging CAM Gopal SV .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which set of chronic conditions does the lecture link to obesity?","choices":["Vitamin toxicity and dehydration","Cardiovascular disease, hypertension, diabetes, kidney failure, joint problems, and some cancers","Only temporary fatigue with no chronic disease risk","Improved cardiovascular and joint health"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the lecture ties obesity to cardiovascular disease, hypertension, diabetes, kidney failure, arthritis/joint failure, and some cancers. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 31"},{"q":"Which bariatric procedure creates a small stomach pouch and reroutes food to bypass part of the small intestine, per lecture?","choices":["Vertical sleeve gastrectomy","AspireAssist®","Gastric bypass","Laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gastric bypass creates a small pouch that limits stomach capacity, and food bypasses a portion of the small intestine, reducing calorie and nutrient absorption. — Weight Control Slides Only - CAM - Putnam .pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is the difference between peripheral (PPN) and total (TPN) parenteral nutrition?","choices":["PPN is restricted to use only in pediatric patients under age five; TPN is restricted to use only in adult patients over sixty-five.","PPN and TPN are simply two different names describing the exact same peripheral-line infusion procedure and nutrient formula.","PPN requires surgically placed central venous access; TPN can be delivered safely through a simple peripheral IV line.","PPN is delivered through a peripheral line for a few days to 2 weeks with limited osmolarity/calories; TPN needs central access but can provide full nutritional needs."],"answer":3,"exp":"PPN uses a peripheral venous line, limited to a few days to 2 weeks with restricted osmolarity and calorie/protein/micronutrient delivery; TPN requires central access but can meet full nutritional needs. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The lecture classifies palm, palm kernel, and coconut oils (\"tropical oils\") as which type of fat?","choices":["Omega-3 polyunsaturated fat","Trans fat produced by hydrogenation","Saturated fat","Monounsaturated fat"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: tropical oils such as palm and coconut are considered saturated fats. — Macro/Micro lecture, slide 59"},{"q":"Which short-term enteral access route is typically used for less than 4-12 weeks, per lecture?","choices":["Surgically or endoscopically placed gastrostomy (GT) tube","Nasogastric (NGT) or nasoduodenal/nasojejunal (NDT/NJT) tube","Combined gastrojejunostomy (GJT) tube requiring surgical placement","Surgically placed jejunostomy (JT) tube for direct small-bowel access"],"answer":1,"exp":"Nasogastric (NGT) or nasoduodenal/nasojejunal (NDT/NJT) tubes are the short-term access route, used for less than 4-12 weeks. — Special Topics in Nutrition for Nova 2026 NEW.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which symptom cluster is listed under nonceliac gluten sensitivity in the lecture?","choices":["Sudden anaphylaxis and shock","Diverticula pouch formation","Headache, muscle pain, and joint pain","Gallstone formation in the gallbladder"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists 'headache, muscle pain, joint pain' among the symptoms of nonceliac gluten sensitivity, along with GI distress, fatigue, weakness, and sleep disorders. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A patient weighs 220 pounds. Using the conversion that 1 kilogram equals about 2.2 pounds, approximately what is that weight in kilograms?","choices":["120 kg","110 kg","100 kg","90 kg"],"answer":2,"exp":"220 lb ÷ 2.2 = 100 kg, matching the lecture's instruction to divide pounds by 2.2 to convert to kilograms. — Human Nutrition lecture, slide 26 (Metric System Summary)"}]};
